<<<
+ is the molecule. When chemical phenomena occur the molecule may be divided into atoms; & these atoms, in the presence of electrical phenomena, may themselves be further divided into electrons or corpuscles.’
===
+ is the molecule. When chemical phenomena occur the molecule may be divided into *atoms*; & these atoms, in the presence of electrical phenomena, may themselves be further divided into *electrons or corpuscles*.’
>>>

<<<
mollify. For inflexions, see VERBS IN -IE, -Y, -YE, 6.
===
**mollify.** For inflexions, see ^^VERBS IN -IE, -Y, -YE^^, 6.
>>>

<<<
Molly. So spelt; see -ry, -I£, -y.
===
**Molly.** So spelt; see ^^-EY, -IE, -Y^^.
>>>

<<<
molten. See MELT.
===
**molten.** See ^^MELT^^.
>>>

<<<
momentarily, momently. The first means for a moment (he was momentarily abashed), the second from moment to moment or every moment (am momently expecting a wire from him).
===
**momentarily, momently.** The first means for a moment (*he was momentarily abashed*), the second from moment to moment or every moment (*am momently expecting a wire from him*).
>>>

<<<
The differentiation is well worth more faithful observance than it gets; & the substitution of either, which sometimes occurs, for instantly or immediately or at once is foolish NovELTY-HUNTING.
===
The differentiation is well worth more faithful observance than it gets; & the substitution of either, which sometimes occurs, for *instantly* or *immediately* or *at once* is foolish ^^NOVELTY-HUNTING^^.
>>>

<<<
momentary, momentous. The first means lasting only for a moment, or transitory; the second means of moment, i, e. of great consequence.
===
**momentary, momentous.** The first means lasting only for a moment, or transitory; the second means of moment, i. e. of great consequence.
>>>

<<<
momentum. PI. usually -ta.
===
**momentum.** Pl. usually *-ta*.
>>>

<<<
monachal, monastic, monkish. Each has its own abstract noun—monachism, monasticism, monkery. Of the three sets monastic(ism) is the one that suits all contexts;
===
**monachal, monastic, monkish.** Each has its own abstract noun—*monachism, monasticism, monkery*. Of the three sets *monastic(ism)* is the one that suits all contexts;
>>>

<<<
it is useful that monkish & monkery should also exist, as serving the purpose of those who wish to adopt a certain tone. Monachal & monachism,
===
it is useful that *monkish* & *monkery* should also exist, as serving the purpose of those who wish to adopt a certain tone. *Monachal* & *monachism*,
>>>

<<<
though they would have passed well enough if monastic(ism) did not exist & were not much better known, seem asit is to have no recommendation
===
though they would have passed well enough if *monastic(ism)* did not exist & were not much better known, seem as it is to have no recommendation
>>>

<<<
those generally current, even though the meaning is the same. If that is, on the contrary, a bad thing, monachal & monachism should be allowed to die. monadism)(monism. Both terms
===
those generally current, even though the meaning is the same. If that is, on the contrary, a bad thing, *monachal* & *monachism* should be allowed to die.

**monadism)(monism.** Both terms
>>>

<<<
called monism (see also META- PHYSICS). Monadism is the name given to a particular form of monism, corresponding to the molecular or atomic theory of matter (see MOLE- CULE), & holding
===
called *monism* (see also ^^METAPHYSICS^^). *Monadism* is the name given to a particular form of *monism*, corresponding to the molecular or atomic theory of matter (see ^^MOLECULE^^), & holding
>>>

<<<
that the universal substance (according to the third variety of monism described in the previous sentence) consists of units called monads.
===
that the universal substance (according to the third variety of *monism* described in the previous sentence) consists of units called *monads*.
>>>

<<<
monarchical, -chic, -chal, -chial. The first is the current form; -ckic is occasionally used for antithetic purposes (the monarchic, the aristocratic, & the democratic branches of our constitution);
===
**monarchical, -chic, -chal, -chial.** The first is the current form; *-chic* is occasionally used for antithetic purposes (*the monarchic, the aristocratic, & the democratic branches of our constitution*);
>>>

<<<
-chal with a slight rhetorical difference, where kingly might serve (the royal harangue has a certain monarchal tone); -ial seems superfluous.
===
*-chal* with a slight rhetorical difference, where *kingly* might serve (*the royal harangue has a certain monarchal tone*); *-ial* seems superfluous.
>>>

<<<
monastic(ism). See MONACHAL,
===
**monastic(ism).** See ^^MONACHAL^^.
>>>

